  "verbalization": "Moexipril, Spirapril, and Trandolapril are all members of the ACE inhibitor class of drugs, which share a common structural feature of being analogs of the active metabolite of enalapril. This structural similarity allows Moexipril to bind to the same target as Spirapril and Trandolapril, and it is this shared structural profile that underpins their pharmacological activity. The evidence suggests that Moexipril, through its resemblance to other ACE inhibitors, binds to Slc15a2, a gene involved in the transport of certain molecules across cellular membranes. The lowest common ancestors of these relationships—such as the Peptide and Ace Inhibitor classes—highlight a broader functional context in which these drugs operate. The binding of Moexipril to Slc15a2 is likely mediated through a common mechanism, possibly involving interactions with the same molecular targets or pathways across these related compounds. The evidence supports a role for Moexipril in modulating Slc15a2 function, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
